  "raw_response": "{\n  \"title\": \"Distance Criterion Example: 20-pip vs 60-pip Impulse Moves Invalidating Change of Character\",\n  \"school\": \"Smart Money Concepts (SMC)\",\n  \"asset\": \"EURUSD\",\n  \"timeframe\": \"1H\",\n  \"market_context\": \"Imperfect uptrend with choppy price action; analyst evaluates whether a recent swing high qualifies as a major swing to determine if a break above it constitutes a valid change of character.\",\n  \"key_observation\": \"The recent impulsive move pushed only 20 pips, while the prior impulsive move was over 60 pips; insufficient distance means the recent swing high is not a major swing, so breaking above it does not signal a true reversal.\",\n  \"analysis_steps\": [\n    \"Step 1: Analyst identifies a recent downward impulsive move that created a new swing high and broke structure to the downside.\",\n    \"Step 2: Analyst measures the distance of the prior impulsive move (over 60 pips) and compares it to the recent impulsive move (only 20 pips).\",\n    \"Step 3: Analyst applies the distance criterion: a swing qualifies as major only if the impulse distance is at least half of the previous impulsive move’s distance.\",\n    \"Step 4: Analyst concludes the recent swing high fails the distance test, so it is not a major swing, and thus a break above it does not constitute a valid change of character.\"\n  ],\n  \"outcome\": null,\n  \"lessons\": \"A swing point must satisfy three criteria—break of structure, imbalance (FVG), and sufficient impulse distance (≥50% of prior impulse)—to be considered ",
